Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||06-08-2000||04:10 PM||||Hey guys and gals. I'm going to pick up my new used fiero either tomorrow of monday (damn slow arse banks). The only problem is, the car has been sitting for 2 years, and the gas is well, you can imagine. I called up some local shops and I was told not to drive it w/ that anchient gas in there, that it would eat into the fuel system. So, my question is, where is the easiest point to drain a gas tank from on an '88 formula? We'll only have basic car jacks, so we won't be able to get too deep in... It's just that it's like a 45 minute drive back up here and it would cost a ton to get it towed home. Any ideas?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||06-08-2000||04:15 PM||||<BR>Do you have any friends with trucks and flat bed trailers, I am sure they can help you get it home.  My in(out)-laws have helped me move my fieros several times.<P>If it is too much to tow, try renting a u-haul truck and flat bed combo, and drag that sucker home.<P>I would not attempt to start or drive that Fiero, until you have spent time working on it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||06-08-2000||05:20 PM||||If old gas is the only problem, I don't think I'd worry too much about it.  The Grape Ape sat for over 6 months before I got it home and running.  I just filled the tank and drove.  I'll be changing the fuel filter next week sometime, but I haven't had any problems so far. :::crosses fingers:::||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||06-09-2000||12:18 PM||||If you're worried about it, pretend that you're a criminal and siphon the gas out.  A jerry can of gas would probably thin out what's left in the tank to a harmless level of crap.  Maybe use a fuel additive before replacing the filter and spark plugs.  Well that's my two cents.  I don't know if that's a good idea, but it sounds good to me. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
